clearcote: authenticated SOCKS5 (RFC 1929)

Chromium offers exactly one SOCKS5 auth method -- 0x00, "no authentication" -- so an authenticated
SOCKS5 proxy, which is what most residential vendors sell, fails with ERR_SOCKS_CONNECTION_FAILED.
curl succeeds against the same endpoint because curl implements RFC 1929.

socks5_client_socket now offers [0x00, 0x02] when credentials are present and implements the
username/password sub-negotiation in four new states between the greeting and the handshake. With
no credentials the greeting and state machine are byte-for-byte what they were.

Credentials arrive on --socks5-credentials=user:pass. utility_process_host.cc must forward it:
the SOCKS5 handshake runs in the NETWORK SERVICE, so without that the switch is invisible where it
is read and every authenticated proxy still fails -- the first build of this change did exactly
that. Patch 210 added the fingerprint switches to the same allowlist for the same reason.

Verified on Windows and Linux against a real authenticated endpoint: 3/3 runs each exit via the
proxy, and the negative control without credentials is still refused, which is what proves the
credentials did the work. WebRTC re-checked with the proxy live: no UDP escapes, no real address in
any candidate, ICE shape unchanged.

--- a/net/socket/socks5_client_socket.h
+++ b/net/socket/socks5_client_socket.h
@@ -122,6 +122,11 @@
   int DoGreetReadComplete(int result);
   int DoGreetWrite();
   int DoGreetWriteComplete(int result);
+  // clearcote: RFC 1929.
+  int DoAuthWrite();
+  int DoAuthWriteComplete(int result);
+  int DoAuthRead();
+  int DoAuthReadComplete(int result);
 
   // Creates a DrainableIOBuffer containing the SOCKS handshake.
   scoped_refptr<DrainableIOBuffer> BuildHandshakeWriteBuffer() const;
--- a/net/socket/socks5_client_socket.cc
+++ b/net/socket/socks5_client_socket.cc
@@ -225,6 +225,27 @@
         net_log_.EndEventWithNetErrorCode(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_GREET_READ,
                                           rv);
         break;
+      // clearcote: RFC 1929 states, reusing the greet net-log events.
+      case STATE_AUTH_WRITE:
+        DCHECK_EQ(OK, rv);
+        net_log_.BeginEvent(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_GREET_WRITE);
+        rv = DoAuthWrite();
+        break;
+      case STATE_AUTH_WRITE_COMPLETE:
+        rv = DoAuthWriteComplete(rv);
+        net_log_.EndEventWithNetErrorCode(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_GREET_WRITE,
+                                          rv);
+        break;
+      case STATE_AUTH_READ:
+        DCHECK_EQ(OK, rv);
+        net_log_.BeginEvent(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_GREET_READ);
+        rv = DoAuthRead();
+        break;
+      case STATE_AUTH_READ_COMPLETE:
+        rv = DoAuthReadComplete(rv);
+        net_log_.EndEventWithNetErrorCode(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_GREET_READ,
+                                          rv);
+        break;
       case STATE_HANDSHAKE_WRITE:
         DCHECK_EQ(OK, rv);
         net_log_.BeginEvent(NetLogEventType::SOCKS5_HANDSHAKE_WRITE);
--- a/content/browser/service_host/utility_process_host.cc
+++ b/content/browser/service_host/utility_process_host.cc
@@ -401,6 +401,11 @@
       // the network fingerprint silently stays stock.
       switches::kFingerprint,
       switches::kFingerprintTlsProfile,
+      // clearcote: RFC 1929 SOCKS5 credentials. The SOCKS5 handshake runs in the
+      // network service, so without this the switch is invisible where it is read
+      // (net/socket/socks5_client_socket.cc) and every authenticated proxy fails
+      // with ERR_SOCKS_CONNECTION_FAILED.
+      "socks5-credentials",
       network::switches::kAdditionalTrustTokenKeyCommitments,
       network::switches::kForceEffectiveConnectionType,
       network::switches::kHostResolverRules,
